WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.463
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.463 --> 00:00:09.834
Being able to work on group projects,
being able to practice role-plays with my

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:12.910
classmates, being able to provide and
receive direct feedback.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.520
All these require me to
work with other people.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.998
All of these require me to get
outside my comfort zone and learn and

00:00:16.998 --> 00:00:17.727
stretch and grow.

00:00:17.727 --> 00:00:24.768
[MUSIC]

00:00:24.768 --> 00:00:29.305
&gt;&gt; Being a student in my classrooms
at the GSB is not a spectator sport.

00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:34.023
[MUSIC]

00:00:34.023 --> 00:00:37.313
&gt;&gt; Their notion is that, depending on the
kind of class we have, the instructor, and

00:00:37.313 --> 00:00:39.900
the goals, that they pick the pedagogy
that works best for them.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:49.900
[MUSIC]

00:00:56.903 --> 00:00:59.560
&gt;&gt; Professor Grousbeck,
who is a serious entrepreneur,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.772
founded probably 15 companies in his life.

00:01:01.772 --> 00:01:05.849
&gt;&gt; Make sure you understand-
&gt;&gt; Role-playing with him, how to fire and

00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:09.567
hire people, being respectful but
still direct,

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:12.954
was one of the most
amazing experiences I had.

00:01:12.954 --> 00:01:14.426
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:14.426 --> 00:01:15.086
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:01:15.086 --> 00:01:19.077
[MUSIC]

00:01:19.077 --> 00:01:21.462
&gt;&gt; If you're learning something
which is more hardcore,

00:01:21.462 --> 00:01:24.824
you really need a lecture-based approach
to kind of soak in the information.

00:01:24.824 --> 00:01:29.451
&gt;&gt; I teach a class in our core which we
call Foundations on Management Accounting.

00:01:29.451 --> 00:01:32.685
And they wanted to figure out which
product actually makes the money, right,

00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:34.070
they had no idea.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:37.000
Given the nature of the material,
much of what I do is lecture.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.918
&gt;&gt; And then you have all these projects
where you apply this information.

00:01:40.918 --> 00:01:43.149
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Very,

00:01:43.149 --> 00:01:47.015
very transformational idea that came
across in the lecture that I really value.

00:01:47.015 --> 00:01:50.736
[MUSIC]

00:01:50.736 --> 00:01:54.300
&gt;&gt; Feedback is one of the core components
of the Stanford GSB experience.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.820
&gt;&gt; And
you're hit with it really from day one

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.597
&gt;&gt; When this situation occurred,

00:01:58.597 --> 00:02:01.762
here's how you reacted, and
here's how that came off.

00:02:01.762 --> 00:02:05.820
&gt;&gt; And then you receive feedback on
how the feedback you gave landed.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.000
&gt;&gt; What that allowed me to do was go
back and fix those specific behaviors.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.190
&gt;&gt; So if you think about
becoming a manager someday,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.225
that's really important to know,
how your feedback comes across.

00:02:14.225 --> 00:02:18.484
[MUSIC]

00:02:18.484 --> 00:02:21.338
&gt;&gt; From the global study trip, I think the
one thing I'll never forget is probably

00:02:21.338 --> 00:02:23.120
meeting the sitting president of Rwanda.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.580
Paul Kagame, the man who's credited for
ending the genocide.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:30.055
[MUSIC]

00:02:30.055 --> 00:02:32.740
&gt;&gt; The interplay between the two of them,
the back and forth.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.329
And sometimes hearing
the different perspectives

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.870
was hugely helpful in informing
how I think about business.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:42.390
What I want to do, who I want to be,
and how I want to react.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.680
&gt;&gt; The one thing that GSB stresses is that
we don't want to be wedded to any one

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:46.647
particular method.

00:02:46.647 --> 00:02:50.503
[MUSIC]

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.815
&gt;&gt; By far my best experience
was a simulation of the UN with

00:02:53.815 --> 00:02:58.310
Professor Condoleezza Rice,
as part of her global class.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.710
&gt;&gt; It was about the European crisis.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.450
&gt;&gt; We actually represented Russia.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.155
&gt;&gt; I, being from Germany,
had to play Greece against Germany.

00:03:05.155 --> 00:03:08.880
&gt;&gt; 48 hours later, we presented to
the former secretary of the state.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.708
&gt;&gt; It was just, it was amazing.

00:03:10.708 --> 00:03:14.675
[MUSIC]

00:03:14.675 --> 00:03:18.361
&gt;&gt; If you're going to learn how to
exercise judgment in the context of

00:03:18.361 --> 00:03:22.050
complex situation, to me,
cases is the best way to do it.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.010
&gt;&gt; Okay, yeah.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.302
&gt;&gt; What might you do to try to negotiate?

00:03:24.302 --> 00:03:27.364
&gt;&gt; I think that's a way to buy some time.

00:03:27.364 --> 00:03:29.213
&gt;&gt; Buy some time,
how much it going to cost?

00:03:29.213 --> 00:03:32.150
&gt;&gt; We use the case as
a platform to role-play and

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.249
to discuss management issues.

00:03:34.249 --> 00:03:38.465
&gt;&gt; And that is so helpful, because
they bring the context, the situation,

00:03:38.465 --> 00:03:41.398
the milieu that the case
was actually involved in.

00:03:41.398 --> 00:03:44.880
And that's something that's
missing in just case, hard fact.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.800
&gt;&gt; And though we might have studied
the case before with the best

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.330
solution in mind.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:52.842
Once we got there and saw the facts on the
ground, the reality was much different.

00:03:52.842 --> 00:03:55.820
&gt;&gt; Let me also just go
through the question.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.267
What was the problem, quickly?

00:03:57.267 --> 00:03:59.665
[MUSIC]

00:03:59.665 --> 00:04:02.572
&gt;&gt; When I was accepted at Stanford, one of
the first things I did was to figure out

00:04:02.572 --> 00:04:04.159
how I could into Extreme Affordability.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.630
So we come in, and we actually
take all these different ideas.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.380
And when one idea comes in that's
very eccentric or wild and

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.980
loud, it actually spurs an idea that's
a little more acceptable and meaningful.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.479
&gt;&gt; The rest of these are really nice
solutions to ethical problems, but

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:21.480
that one kind of like-
&gt;&gt; And

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.700
then pretty soon we find ourselves
going down this path together,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.850
even though we're from various different
backgrounds, to solve this common need.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.260
I think it's really opened my eyes to some
of the interesting insights that can come

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.900
out of students from the law school,
students from the medical school.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:39.791
It's been fantastic.

00:04:39.791 --> 00:04:44.420
&gt;&gt; The students are always
getting cutting-edge knowledge.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.030
There are always classes being taught here
that would never be taught anywhere else.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.250
Because no one else is even thinking
about the application of what

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.990
we're doing to business.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:57.110
&gt;&gt; I wish I could take all the knowledge
I've learned in the last year and a half.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.169
Have that with me, and go back and
do this experience all over again.

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:03.178
&gt;&gt; There is no question
that it's more engaging.

00:05:03.178 --> 00:05:13.178
[MUSIC]

